extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Data"
	- pagedescription = "Where a chart's data comes from in Tablechart: a table in your HTML, a table from Markdown, or a JSON file."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Data
				p.lead A table in your HTML, a table from Markdown or a JSON file.

	+section("A table")
		p A chart is a #[code figure] with #[code data-chart] and a table in it. Each row of the table is a bar or a point. The first cell of a row is its name, the other cells are its values.
		p A chart is always a #[code figure], with a #[code figcaption] for the title. Then the chart has a name, also for screen readers. If the element is not a figure, the console says so.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apples picked
					br
					span thousands
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p The table stays on the page, but it is hidden for sighted users. Screen readers, search engines and translation scripts can still read it. If JavaScript does not run, the table is all there is.
		p The #[code figcaption] is the title of the chart. Text after a #[code br] is shown as its subtitle. A #[code figcaption] is not needed: you can also put a heading above the figure, and style it like the other headings on your page.

	+section("Several value columns")
		p If the table has more than 1 value column, each column is a line, or a bar in each group. The table then needs a #[code thead]: its headers are the names of the columns.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr><th>Variety</th><th>2022</th><th>2026</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th>Elstar</th><td>118</td><td>131</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>

		p See #[a(href=url('/types/column.html')) Column], #[a(href=url('/types/bar.html')) Bar], #[a(href=url('/types/line.html')) Line] and #[a(href=url('/types/area.html')) Area].

	+section("Attributes on rows and headers")
		table
			thead
				tr
					th Attribute
					th On
					th Does
			tbody
				tr
					td: code data-kind="total"
					td A row
					td Makes the row a total, in a waterfall or a donut
				tr
					td: code class
					td A row
					td Is copied to the bar, the number and the name of the row
				tr
					td: code data-kind="focus"
					td A column header
					td Highlights that line or bar
				tr
					td: code data-marker
					td A column header
					td Sets the point of a line
				tr
					td: code data-line
					td A column header
					td Makes a line dashed or dotted

	+section("A JSON file or endpoint")
		p Add #[code data-chart-src] with the address of a JSON file. Tablechart reads the file and makes a table from it, and then Tablechart creates the chart from that table. If you #[strong also] had an HTML table in there, that is then replaced, unless it has a #[em header row]: see #[a(href="#missing-names") Missing names]. The table is made by JavaScript, so without JavaScript there is no table: see #[a(href=url('/features/accessibility.html')) Accessibility].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-src=url('/data/apples.json'))
				figcaption: b Apples sold by variety

		p A browser cannot read a file from a page that is opened from your computer, with an address that starts with #[code file://]. Open the page from a server.

		h3#json-structure JSON structure
		p The rows can be written in two ways. As a list of rows, each with its #[code label] and its #[code value], or its #[code values] if there are more value columns:

		+codeblock('JSON').
			[
				{ "label": "Jan 2025", "value": "12.4" },
				{ "label": "Feb 2025", "value": "-3.1" },
				{ "label": "Mar 2025", "value": "8.7" }
			]

		p Or as names with their value, or their values:

		+codeblock('JSON').
			{ "Spring": "58", "Summer": "72", "Autumn": "70" }

		+codeblock('JSON').
			{ "Elstar": ["118", "131"], "Jonagold": ["140", "135"], "Braeburn": ["109", "121"] }

		p To name the columns too, put the rows, in either way, in an object with #[code rows]. #[code series] has the names of the value columns, and #[code label] the name of the first column:

		+codeblock('JSON').
			{
				"label": "Variety",
				"series": ["2022", "2026"],
				"rows": [
					{ "label": "Elstar", "values": ["118", "131"] },
					{ "label": "Jonagold", "values": ["140", "135"] },
					{ "label": "Braeburn", "values": ["109", "121"] }
				]
			}

		p Write a value as a string, such as #[code "1.0"], to keep its decimals. A row in a list with #[code "kind": "total"] is a total. In names with values, names that are whole numbers, such as #[code "2022"], come first and from low to high, whatever their order in the file. Other names keep their order.

		h3#missing-names Missing names

		p If the file has only rows (so data only, like the top 3 examples above), without #[code label] and #[code series], the table has no header row, and screen readers cannot say what the columns are.

		p Then write just the header row in the figure yourself, in a table with only a #[code thead]. Tablechart keeps it and adds the rows from the file. A header row in the figure goes before the names in the file. Screen readers use the header row to name the columns, and with #[code data-i18n] on a #[code th], a translation script translates it.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<figure data-chart="column" data-chart-src="/api/profit-per-month/">
				<figcaption><b>Profit per month</b></figcaption>
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr><th>Month</th><th>Profit</th></tr>
					</thead>
				</table>
			</figure>

	+section("A table from Markdown")
		p If your Markdown tool can add attributes to a table, add #[code data-chart] to the table. This example uses #[a(href="https://github.com/arve0/markdown-it-attrs") markdown-it-attrs], which reads the attributes from the line after the table.

		+codeblock('Markdown').
			: **Apples picked**\
			thousands

			| Season | Apples |
			| ------ | ------ |
			| Spring | 58     |
			| Summer | 72     |
			| Autumn | 70     |
			{data-chart=column}

		p Markdown has no #[code figcaption], so a paragraph that starts with #[code ": "] just before or after the table is the title. A line break in it starts the subtitle. Tablechart puts the table and the title together in a #[code div] with the chart attributes.
		p You can also leave out the #[code ": "] paragraph and write the title as a heading above the table, such as #[code #### Apples picked].

		.card
			.demo
				div
					p
						| :#{' '}
						strong Apples picked
						br
						| thousands
					table(data-chart="column")
						thead
							tr
								th Season
								th Apples
						tbody
							each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70']]
								tr
									td= row[0]
									td= row[1]

		p In the first column, a name in #[code **bold**] makes the row a total. In the header, a name in bold is highlighted.

	+section("Next")
		p To change how the numbers are shown, see #[a(href=url('/features/numbers.html')) Numbers].
